{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radiological units measure different quantities radiological units measure different quantities","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","fileName":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Algebraic SI Derived Units","Dimensional Homogeneity in Equations","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radiological units measure different quantities radiological units measure different quantities","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","fileName":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities","heading":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Pryor gives radionuclide activity, absorbed dose, dose equivalent, and X/gamma-ray exposure separate conversion categories. Activity is represented by curie or becquerel and has inverse-time units; absorbed dose by rad or gray has energy-per-mass units; dose equivalent by rem or sievert is another named category; exposure by roentgen or coulomb per kilogram is charge-per-mass. A factor valid inside one pair does not convert a different quantity merely because both belong to radiology. See [[Algebraic SI Derived Units]] and [[Dimensional Homogeneity in Equations]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Algebraic SI Derived Units","Dimensional Homogeneity in Equations","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"radiological units measure different quantities radiological units measure different quantities","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","fileName":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Radiological Units Measure Different Quantities","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Algebraic SI Derived Units","Dimensional Homogeneity in Equations","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}